A 400 number is a perfect square N = m², where m has exactly two distinct prime factors. All exponents in N are even exactly when N is a square, and N has the same prime factors as m. So the function walks m down from isqrt(a) until it finds such an m. Since m ≤ 10^6, trial division by primes up to 1000 is enough.

